          "tool_use_id": "toolu_0133nAwS59rtsAXyV9ZByheU",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/security-basics.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Security Basics\\ndescription: Fundamental principles and practices for secure file handling and input validation.\\ntimestamp: 2025-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Security\\n  - pattern/Input-Validation\\n  - pattern/Least-Privilege\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Established security principles from standard practice; single source for formalized checklist.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nAlways scope file writes to approved wiki paths.\\n\\n## Mitigation Checklist\\n\\nA structured approach to secure file and data handling:\\n\\n1. **Principle of Least Privilege** \\u2014 Grant write scope only to the paths a process genuinely needs. Do not assume broad permissions; restrict authority to the minimum required for the operation to succeed.\\n\\n2. **Input Validation at Trust Boundaries** \\u2014 Treat all external content as untrusted data, never as instructions. Validate and sanitize all inputs before processing, and maintain clear boundaries between trusted and untrusted domains.\\n\\n3. **Path Canonicalization** \\u2014 Resolve and validate file paths before any write to block directory traversal attacks. Normalize paths to their canonical form and verify they remain within the intended write scope.\\n\\n4. **Audit Logging** \\u2014 Record every denied action with its reason for later review. Maintain detailed logs of security decisions, rejections, and boundary violations to enable post-incident analysis and continuous improvement.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Security best practices for file system access and input handling, applied to wiki ingest processes.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
